{"id":190901442634423,"type":"album","name":"Nothingface","artist":"Voivod","year":1989,"weight":16,"info":{"cover":"https://dn721602.ca.archive.org/0/items/mbid-626e981b-5c94-43c4-bfb4-50b2c3cffe4a/mbid-626e981b-5c94-43c4-bfb4-50b2c3cffe4a-31763392250.jpg","kind":"Album","tracks":9,"scaruffi":7.5,"yt":"R5TnV09-n9A","links":{"spotify":"https://open.spotify.com/album/49JPEgPvEaFp89xqzeQvF7","youtube":"https://music.youtube.com/playlist?list=OLAK5uy_kbqAv3LtrVlaoJ9bSauzYsur2GJnPiIcw","rateyourmusic":"https://rateyourmusic.com/release/album/voivod/nothingface"},"desc":"Nothingface is the fifth studio album by Canadian heavy metal band Voivod. It was released by Mechanic/MCA Records on 1989. The album marked a change for the band, expanding their music into a more progressive rock/metal sound. Several riffs are heavily influenced by Igor Stravinsky's \"The Rite of Spring\", specifically the centre section of \"Pre-Ignition\". A music video made for the album's third track, a cover of Pink Floyd's \"Astronomy Domine\", received airplay on MTV's Headbangers Ball. The sound on Nothingface is rooted in progressive rock, and incorporates elements of thrash metal and space rock. Vocal melody is heavily emphasized ."},"artist_ref":{"id":83213005995646,"name":"Voivod","info":{"img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/W0937-Hellfest2013_Voivod_70864_%28cropped%29.JPG?width=500"}},"labels":[],"genres":[{"id":174164112458259,"type":"genre","name":"Heavy Metal","artist":null,"year":null,"weight":782,"yt":null,"src":"musthear"},{"id":190704175116840,"type":"genre","name":"Progressive Metal","artist":null,"year":null,"weight":268,"yt":null,"src":"musthear,rym"},{"id":169159335096911,"type":"genre","name":"Avant-Garde Metal","artist":null,"year":null,"weight":228,"yt":null,"src":"rym"}],"lists":[{"id":178023330130382,"name":"RYM Top — Rock — Avant-Garde Metal","src":"musthear","grp":"rateyourmusic","list_year":null,"rank":9,"year":null,"note":null,"total":200,"scaruff